Well I got a few responses I have included below.  It seems to be a problem
w/ the tape drive itself.  I'm going to see if the kzpsa is up to its new
firmware or not and try again.  The drive worked fine on some VAX's not
more that a few weeks ago.

Thanks for all the replies,

Joe

Replies:
----
From: <alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com>

	The lower levels of the CAM SCSI driver allow the higher
	levels to provide a pointer to space for a Request Sense
	buffer in the data structure used to send commands.  If
	a particular command fails, the lower levels of the driver
	will automatically do a Request Sense command to find out
	what caused the error.  In this case, it would appear that
	the Request Sense failed.  From the look of it, the device
	isn't talking anymore.

--
From: "Dr. Tom Blinn, 603-881-0646" <tpb@zk3.dec.com>

> Does anyone know what a CAM SCSI error "AutoSense Failed" on a tape drive
can mean?
> 
> I have included the uerf logs.  Any help at all would be appreciated.

It means your tape drive is misbehaving.  You have some hard errors logged
as well.  It looks like you got a hard error from the drive, and during the
attempted recovery, some operations failed.

Looks like unit 6 on your second SCSI bus (bus 1).  

Since it's impossible to tell from the UERF output what type of drive you've
got plugged in, it's hard to be more specific.  Tape drives are more messy
than disk drives, and to know exactly what a given SCSI sequence does, you
often need to know what kind of tape drive you're dealing with.
 
--
From: "Dr. Tom Blinn, 603-881-0646" <tpb@zk3.dec.com>

> This is a TAPE Express 3480 drive.
> 
> What is wierd is the message doesn't happen on every read.  Could it be a
> problem w/ the drive head?
> 
> The drive is a couple years old and has been used pretty heavily.

Hard to say.  I have no particular experience with that particular model or
with the 3480 style drives in general.  If there are cleaning cartridges for
it, I'd run a cleaning cartridge cycle, and if there are diagnostics for it
I'd want to run the vendor diagnostics (wishful thinking, I realize).  Take
a look at the tapex reference page for the "out of the box" UNIX exerciser,
or look at what's in the VET package on the firmware CDROM.  But either way
it is likely to be the drive acting up.  I just don't have practical advice
based on experience with that drive model.

Tom
 
--

